# Git commits

## When commits are allowed

- **Create a git commit only when the user explicitly asks** (e.g. “commit this”, “make a commit”, “git commit”). Phrases like “save my work” or “checkpoint” count as explicit only if they clearly mean a **git commit**—when in doubt, **ask** before committing.
- **Do not commit** to “helpfully” save progress, to finish a task, or because a change set “feels done.” **Never** run `git commit` without a clear, direct request to commit.
- **Do not** amend, rebase, or otherwise rewrite history unless the user explicitly asks.

## Push and branches

- **Do not** `git push` (or force-push) unless the user **explicitly** asks to push.
- **Do not** merge or rebase branches unless the user **explicitly** asks (same spirit as commits).

### Branch model (this project)

| Branch | Role |
|--------|------|
| **`master`** | **Root / release line.** Stable, production-ready history. Does **not** receive direct feature work. Updates from **`dev`** follow the **`dev` → `master`** subsection below (compatibility + pull request). |
| **`dev`** | **Integration branch.** Collects completed work from **feature** branches. Merge feature branches into `dev` only after **checking compatibility** with current `dev` (build runs, conflicts resolved, smoke test as the user defines). |
| **`feature/<name>`** | **Feature development.** One branch per feature or task slice (e.g. `feature/ticket-filters`, `security/pnpm-audit-fixes`). Open from up-to-date **`dev`**, implement the feature, then merge into **`dev`** when ready—not directly into `master`. |

**Intended flow:** `feature/...` (or similarly named work branches) → **`dev`** (integrate & verify) → **`master`** (release). Hotfixes from `master` are **out of scope** unless the user explicitly asks—then follow their instructions.

### `dev` → `master` (release only when compatible + pull request)

- **`dev` may be merged into `master` only when both are true:** (1) **`dev` is compatible** with release expectations—build succeeds, conflicts resolved, and any checks the user cares about (tests, CI, smoke paths) have been satisfied; (2) the integration happens through a **pull request** (`dev` → `master`) on the hosting platform (GitHub, GitLab, Azure DevOps, etc.), reviewed and merged there—not as an informal local-only merge by default.
- **Agent behavior:** Do **not** run or recommend `git checkout master && git merge dev` (or equivalent) as the **normal** release path. Instead, tell the user to **open and complete a PR** from `dev` into `master` after compatibility is confirmed. A **local** merge of `dev` into `master` is acceptable **only** if the user **explicitly** asks for it **and** states that the **PR has already been merged** on the remote (e.g. updating local `master` to match) **or** that they waive PR for this repo and still confirm **compatibility**—when in doubt, **ask**.
- **No remote / no PR host:** If the project has no Git hosting, a formal PR may be impossible. In that case, **`dev` → `master` still requires** an **explicit user request** plus **stated compatibility** before any merge; the agent must **not** merge silently. Suggest adding a remote + PR workflow when the user wants enforcement.

**Agent defaults when the user asks for a new branch for work:** create **`feature/<short-descriptive-slug>`** from **`dev`** (after `git fetch` / `checkout dev` / `pull` if the user’s workflow uses a remote), unless the user specifies another name or base (e.g. a `security/` or `chore/` branch they name explicitly).

**Hosting note:** On GitHub/GitLab (etc.), enable **branch protection** on `master` (e.g. require PR, require reviews, require status checks) so **`dev` → `master` cannot bypass** the pull-request rule at the server. This rule guides **agent** behavior even when protection is not yet configured.

## Staging scope

- **Stage only** paths the user named, or changes that clearly belong to the requested commit. If the working tree has **other** modified or untracked files, **list them** and either leave them unstaged or **ask** whether to include them—**do not** silently `git add -A` unless the user explicitly asked to commit **everything**.
- Before committing, prefer **`git status`** (or equivalent) so staged vs unstaged is intentional.

## One logical change per commit

- Prefer **one coherent story** per commit. If the working tree mixes unrelated changes (e.g. a **BUGFIX** and a **DOCS** refresh with no link), **tell the user** and offer **split commits** (stage subsets with separate messages) or **ask** which single prefix and scope they want for this commit.

## Safety (never commit)

- **Secrets:** Do not commit API keys, passwords, tokens, private keys, or full `.env` files that hold secrets. If such material appears staged or in diffs, **stop**, warn the user, and remove it from the index before proceeding.
- **Generated and ignored artifacts:** Do not add paths that are **build output**, package manager or editor cache, or already listed in `.gitignore` (e.g. `target/`, `node_modules/`, `.next/`, `dist/`, coverage output) unless the user **explicitly** asks to track that path anyway.

## Hooks

- **Do not** bypass Git hooks with `--no-verify` / `-n` unless the user **explicitly** asks to skip hooks for that commit.

